SAT_CHUNK("rw/synthesis/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.06e9a7629f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-29","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is located on a remote Norwegian island.</li><li>It stores backup copies of seeds from gene banks around the world.</li><li>It opened in 2008.</li><li>In 2015, scientists withdrew seeds to rebuild a gene bank damaged by the war in Syria.</li><li>This was the first withdrawal in the vault's history.</li><li>The vault is built into a mountain to keep seeds frozen even without electricity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize the vault's practical value.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Built into a mountain, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault can keep seeds frozen even without electricity, and it stores backups from gene banks worldwide.","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault opened in 2008, and its first withdrawal took place seven years later, in 2015.","Opened in 2008 on a remote Norwegian island, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault stores backup copies of seeds from gene banks around the world.","The vault's backup seeds have already proved useful: in 2015, scientists withdrew seeds to rebuild a gene bank damaged by the war in Syria."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Practical value is best shown by an actual use: the 2015 withdrawal to rebuild Syria's gene bank. The other choices describe the vault's location, design, or dates. The dates choice mentions the withdrawal but not its purpose, so it doesn't show value."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.aeb80fc6b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-30","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maria Sibylla Merian was a German naturalist and artist.</li><li>In 1699, she traveled to Suriname to study insects.</li><li>Most naturalists at the time studied preserved specimens in Europe.</li><li>Merian observed living insects in their habitats.</li><li>Her 1705 book illustrated the complete life cycles of insects on their food plants.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how Merian's approach differed from that of other naturalists.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["While most naturalists of her time studied preserved specimens in Europe, Merian traveled to Suriname to observe living insects in their habitats.","Merian, a German naturalist and artist, traveled to Suriname in 1699 and published a book in 1705 illustrating insects on their food plants.","Merian's 1705 book illustrated the complete life cycles of the insects she studied in Suriname, showing each one on the plants that it fed on, an approach that made the book famous.","In 1699, the German naturalist and artist Maria Sibylla Merian traveled to Suriname to study insects."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal calls for a contrast between Merian and her contemporaries. Only one choice sets living insects in their habitats against preserved specimens studied by \"most naturalists.\" The others accurately describe Merian's trip or book but never mention how other naturalists worked."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.2ae9097f06","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-31","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Researchers studied two groups of students preparing for a biology exam.</li><li>Group A reread their notes several times.</li><li>Group B tested themselves with practice questions.</li><li>Both groups studied for the same total amount of time.</li><li>Group B scored an average of 18 percentage points higher on the exam.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's findings to an audience unfamiliar with the study.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Students who tested themselves with practice questions scored 18 percentage points higher on average than students in the other group.","In a study of students preparing for a biology exam, those who tested themselves with practice questions outscored those who reread their notes by 18 percentage points on average, despite studying for the same amount of time.","Researchers compared two study methods, rereading notes and self-testing, among students preparing for a biology exam.","Group B, which used practice questions, scored an average of 18 percentage points higher than Group A."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An unfamiliar audience needs the context (a biology exam), both methods, the equal study time, and the result. The short \"other group\" choice leaves out what that group did. The comparison choice gives no results. The Group A/Group B choice uses labels the audience wouldn't know."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.485a639c34","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-32","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Aswan High Dam in Egypt was completed in 1970.</li><li>It controls flooding along the Nile River.</li><li>It generates hydroelectric power for millions of people.</li><li>It traps silt that once fertilized farmland downstream.</li><li>Farmers now rely heavily on chemical fertilizers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both a benefit and a drawback of the dam.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Aswan High Dam traps silt that once fertilized farmland, and as a result, farmers downstream now rely heavily on chemical fertilizers.","Although the Aswan High Dam generates hydroelectric power for millions of people, it traps silt that once fertilized farmland downstream.","Completed in 1970, the Aswan High Dam controls flooding along the Nile River and generates hydroelectric power for millions of people.","Farmers along the Nile rely heavily on chemical fertilizers, which have replaced the silt once carried downstream before 1970."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal needs one benefit and one drawback. Only the correct choice pairs power generation (benefit) with trapped silt (drawback). The flood-control choice gives two benefits. The fertilizer choices give only drawbacks or their effects."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.619b005439","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-33","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Japanese art of kintsugi repairs broken pottery.</li><li>Artisans join the pieces with lacquer mixed with powdered gold.</li><li>The repaired cracks remain visible.</li><li>The practice reflects the idea that an object's history, including damage, adds to its beauty.</li><li>Kintsugi may have begun in the late 1400s.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain the idea behind kintsugi.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In kintsugi, a practice that may have begun in the late 1400s, artisans repair broken pottery by joining the pieces with lacquer mixed with powdered gold so the cracks remain visible.","Kintsugi, which leaves repaired cracks visible, reflects the idea that an object's history, including damage, adds to its beauty.","Using lacquer mixed with powdered gold, kintsugi artisans repair pottery so that the cracks remain visible.","Kintsugi, a Japanese art that may have begun in the late 1400s, repairs broken pottery with gold-flecked lacquer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal is the idea behind the practice, so the choice must state its meaning: that history and damage add beauty. The others describe the method or origin. Visible cracks alone don't state the idea."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.dd40ccc228","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-34","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ada Lovelace was an English mathematician.</li><li>In 1843, she published notes on Charles Babbage's proposed Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer.</li><li>Babbage's machine was never built.</li><li>Lovelace's notes included a method for calculating Bernoulli numbers with the machine.</li><li>Many historians consider this method the first published computer program.</li><li>Lovelace suggested the machine might someday compose music.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize Lovelace's significance in the history of computing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In 1843, the English mathematician Ada Lovelace published a method for calculating Bernoulli numbers using the Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer designed by Charles Babbage that was never built.","Lovelace, an English mathematician, suggested that Babbage's Analytical Engine might someday compose music.","Lovelace's 1843 notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine included what many historians consider the first published computer program.","Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer, was never built, though Lovelace published notes on it in 1843."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Significance is shown by the historians' judgment that her notes contained the first published computer program. The Bernoulli choice describes the method but leaves out why it matters. The music choice is interesting but not the basis of her significance, and the never-built choice focuses on the machine."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.b039cd32e1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-35","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Venus and Earth are similar in size.</li><li>Venus's surface temperature is about 465°C.</li><li>Earth's average surface temperature is about 15°C.</li><li>Venus has a thick atmosphere made mostly of carbon dioxide.</li><li>This atmosphere traps heat through an extreme greenhouse effect.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain why the two planets have such different temperatures.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Venus has a thick atmosphere made mostly of carbon dioxide, while Earth's average surface temperature is about 15°C.","Although Venus and Earth are similar in size, Venus's surface temperature of about 465°C is far higher than Earth's average surface temperature of about 15°C, a difference of roughly 450°C.","Venus's thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat through an extreme greenhouse effect, making its surface about 450°C hotter than Earth's.","Venus and Earth are similar in size, but Venus's surface reaches about 465°C."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal is to explain the difference, so the choice must give a cause: the heat-trapping atmosphere. The size contrast choices describe the difference without explaining it. The mixed choice places an atmosphere fact next to a temperature figure without connecting them."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.4370fe762f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-36","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Harlem Renaissance was an African American cultural movement.</li><li>It flourished in New York City in the 1920s.</li><li>Zora Neale Hurston was a writer and anthropologist.</li><li>Hurston collected folklore in the American South.</li><li>She used this folklore in her novel Their Eyes Were Watching God (1937).</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain how Hurston's work as an anthropologist influenced her fiction.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["As an anthropologist, Hurston collected folklore in the American South during the years of the Harlem Renaissance.","In 1937, Hurston, who had also worked as an anthropologist, published her novel Their Eyes Were Watching God.","Hurston, a writer and anthropologist, was part of the Harlem Renaissance, which flourished in New York City in the 1920s.","Hurston drew on the folklore she had collected in the American South as an anthropologist when writing her novel Their Eyes Were Watching God."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal links her anthropology to her fiction. Only the correct choice says she used collected folklore in her novel. The 1937 choice mentions both roles but not the connection. The folklore-collecting choice doesn't mention her fiction."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.62911372ef","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-37","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Honeybees and bumblebees both pollinate crops.</li><li>Honeybees live in colonies of up to 60,000 bees.</li><li>Bumblebee colonies usually contain fewer than 500 bees.</li><li>Bumblebees can fly in cooler temperatures than honeybees can.</li><li>Bumblebees use \"buzz pollination,\" vibrating flowers to release pollen, which benefits crops such as tomatoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize an advantage bumblebees have over honeybees as pollinators.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["While honeybees live in colonies of up to 60,000 bees, bumblebee colonies usually contain fewer than 500 bees.","Honeybees and bumblebees both pollinate crops, though bumblebee colonies are much smaller.","Bumblebees vibrate flowers to release pollen, a technique known as \"buzz pollination\" that benefits crops such as tomatoes, while their colonies usually contain fewer than 500 bees.","Unlike honeybees, bumblebees can fly in cooler temperatures, allowing them to pollinate crops in weather that keeps honeybees grounded."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal is an advantage over honeybees. Flying in cooler weather is explicitly compared with honeybees. Buzz pollination is a strength, but that choice doesn't compare bumblebees with honeybees. Colony size is a difference, not an advantage for pollination."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.1db1752f80","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-38","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mexico City was built on the site of Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital.</li><li>Tenochtitlan was built on an island in Lake Texcoco.</li><li>After the Spanish conquest, the lake was gradually drained.</li><li>Much of the modern city sits on soft former lakebed clay.</li><li>Parts of the city are sinking by up to 50 centimeters per year as groundwater is pumped out.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain why parts of Mexico City are sinking.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["After the Spanish conquest, Lake Texcoco, where the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan once stood on an island, was gradually drained, and much of the modern city now sits on the former lakebed.","Built largely on soft clay from a drained lakebed, parts of Mexico City are sinking by up to 50 centimeters per year as groundwater is pumped out.","Mexico City was built on the site of Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, which stood on an island in Lake Texcoco.","Parts of Mexico City, which was built on the site of the Aztec capital, are sinking by up to 50 centimeters per year."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An explanation needs the causes: soft lakebed clay and groundwater pumping. The history choices give background without the sinking. The sinking choice gives the rate but not the reason."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.a3ded19a54","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-39","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Octavia Butler was an American science fiction writer.</li><li>Her novel Kindred (1979) follows a Black woman from 1970s California who is repeatedly pulled back in time to a plantation in 1810s Maryland.</li><li>Butler said she wanted readers to feel the reality of slavery, not just know about it.</li><li>In 1995, she became the first science fiction writer to receive a MacArthur Fellowship.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain Butler's purpose in writing Kindred.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Octavia Butler, an American science fiction writer, published her novel Kindred in 1979.","In Kindred, Butler sends a modern Black woman back to an 1810s plantation because, as she said, she wanted readers to feel the reality of slavery, not just know about it.","Kindred follows a Black woman from 1970s California who is repeatedly pulled back in time to a plantation in 1810s Maryland.","In 1995, Butler, the author of Kindred, became the first science fiction writer to receive a MacArthur Fellowship."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Purpose is stated in Butler's own explanation: she wanted readers to feel slavery's reality. The plot summary describes the novel but not why she wrote it. The award and publication choices give facts about her career."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.d7c9f1bfea","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-40","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>A \"food desert\" is an area with limited access to affordable, nutritious food.</li><li>In 2017, the town of Carver opened a community-owned grocery store.</li><li>Before the store opened, the nearest supermarket was 14 miles away.</li><li>A year later, residents' reported fruit and vegetable consumption had not changed significantly.</li><li>Researchers suggest that price and habits may matter as much as distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present a finding that complicates a common assumption about food deserts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In 2017, the town of Carver, a food desert whose nearest supermarket was 14 miles away, opened a community-owned grocery store to give residents better access to affordable, nutritious food.","A food desert is an area with limited access to affordable, nutritious food, such as Carver before 2017.","After a grocery store opened in Carver, a food desert, residents' reported fruit and vegetable consumption did not change significantly, suggesting that access alone may not change diets.","Before a community-owned grocery store opened in Carver in 2017, the nearest supermarket was 14 miles away."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The common assumption is that closer stores improve diets. The finding that consumption didn't change after the store opened complicates that. The other choices describe the town's situation or the store's opening but not the unexpected result."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.5faf042174","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-41","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Stonehenge is a prehistoric monument in England.</li><li>Its large sarsen stones came from about 25 kilometers away.</li><li>Its smaller bluestones came from the Preseli Hills in Wales, about 240 kilometers away.</li><li>Moving the bluestones would have required enormous effort.</li><li>Some researchers think the bluestones were valued because of their origin in a place with ancestral significance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize the distance over which some of Stonehenge's stones were moved.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Stonehenge, a prehistoric monument in England, includes both large sarsen stones and smaller bluestones.","Some researchers think Stonehenge's bluestones were valued because they came from a place with ancestral significance.","Some of Stonehenge's stones, the bluestones, were brought from the Preseli Hills in Wales, about 240 kilometers away.","Stonehenge's large sarsen stones came from about 25 kilometers away, a distance that required considerable effort to cover."],"answer":"C","explanation":"To emphasize distance, the choice should give the greatest one: 240 kilometers from Wales. The sarsen choice gives a much shorter distance. The significance choice gives no distance, and the monument choice mentions stone types only."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.918e9517e9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-42","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Scientists studied crows in Seattle.</li><li>Researchers wearing a particular mask captured and banded some crows.</li><li>Afterward, crows scolded people wearing that mask, even when the wearer was not a researcher.</li><li>Crows that had never been captured also scolded the mask.</li><li>The researchers concluded that crows can learn about threats from other crows.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's conclusion and the evidence that supports it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because crows that had never been captured also scolded the mask worn by the researchers who banded crows, scientists concluded that crows can learn about threats from other crows.","Crows in Seattle scolded people wearing a mask that researchers had worn while capturing and banding crows, even when the wearer was not a researcher.","Researchers who captured and banded crows in Seattle wore a particular mask, which crows afterward scolded.","Scientists concluded that crows can learn about threats from other crows after studying crows in Seattle."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal needs both the conclusion and its key support. Crows that had never been captured still scolded the mask, which shows they learned from other crows. One choice gives only evidence, and another only the conclusion. The fourth doesn't mention uncaptured crows, the crucial evidence."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.dc08772664","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-43","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>In 1964, the Tokyo Olympics were broadcast internationally by satellite.</li><li>This was the first time Olympic events were shown live across the Pacific Ocean.</li><li>The broadcast used the satellite Syncom 3.</li><li>Viewers in the United States watched the opening ceremony live.</li><li>Previous Olympics had been shown abroad only on film flown in hours or days later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize what was new about the 1964 broadcast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Unlike earlier Games, which reached viewers abroad only through film flown in hours or days later, the 1964 Tokyo Olympics were broadcast live across the Pacific by satellite.","Using the satellite Syncom 3, broadcasters sent coverage of the 1964 Tokyo Olympics to viewers around the world, and viewers in the United States were able to watch the opening ceremony.","The 1964 Tokyo Olympics were broadcast internationally by satellite, using a satellite called Syncom 3.","Viewers in the United States were able to watch the opening ceremony of the 1964 Tokyo Olympics."],"answer":"A","explanation":"What was new is shown by contrast with the past: live transmission instead of film delivered later. The Syncom 3 choices describe the method without saying what changed. The opening-ceremony choice omits \"live\" and gives no comparison."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.b34bc90ee2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data4-44","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Glass frogs live in Central and South American rainforests.</li><li>Their skin is translucent, especially on their undersides.</li><li>When a glass frog sleeps, it stores most of its red blood cells in its liver.</li><li>This makes the frog up to 61 percent more transparent.</li><li>Researchers believe the transparency helps frogs avoid predators.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe a specific mechanism behind glass frogs' transparency.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Glass frogs can become up to 61 percent more transparent, which may help them avoid predators.","Glass frogs, which live in Central and South American rainforests, have translucent skin, especially on their undersides, and researchers believe this helps them avoid predators.","Researchers believe that the transparency of glass frogs helps them avoid predators while they sleep.","While sleeping, glass frogs store most of their red blood cells in their livers, which makes them up to 61 percent more transparent."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A mechanism explains how the transparency happens: hiding red blood cells in the liver. The 61 percent choice gives the effect without the mechanism. The predator choice gives the purpose, and the skin choice describes appearance."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.f270ba1fca","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-54","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Rosetta Stone was discovered in Egypt in 1799.</li><li>It contains the same decree written in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek.</li><li>Scholars could already read ancient Greek.</li><li>Jean-François Champollion used the Greek text to help decipher the hieroglyphs.</li><li>He announced his breakthrough in 1822.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain why the Rosetta Stone was useful for deciphering hieroglyphs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because the Rosetta Stone repeats the same decree in ancient Greek, which scholars could already read, it gave Champollion a key to deciphering the hieroglyphs.","The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree written in three different scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek.","Discovered in Egypt in 1799, the Rosetta Stone contains a decree written in hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek scripts, which scholars studied for decades.","Champollion announced in 1822 that he had deciphered the hieroglyphs on the Rosetta Stone, which was discovered in Egypt in 1799."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal is to explain why the stone was useful. Only the correct choice connects the repeated decree to the fact that scholars could already read Greek. The other choices describe the stone or Champollion's announcement without explaining the stone's usefulness."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.fe9f6bc790","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-55","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter.</li><li>Temperatures can fall below −40°C.</li><li>Males incubate eggs on their feet for about two months without eating.</li><li>Males huddle together in groups of thousands.</li><li>Penguins in a huddle take turns moving from the cold outer edge to the warmer center.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe a behavior that helps male emperor penguins survive the cold.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Male emperor penguins incubate eggs on their feet for about two months during the Antarctic winter, when temperatures can fall below −40°C.","Male emperor penguins huddle in groups of thousands, taking turns moving from the cold outer edge to the warmer center.","Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter, when temperatures can fall below −40°C.","For about two months, male emperor penguins go without eating while they incubate eggs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a behavior that helps with survival in the cold. Huddling and rotating to the warm center is that behavior. Incubating eggs and fasting are behaviors, but the notes don't present them as ways of coping with cold. Breeding in winter describes the challenge, not a survival behavior."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.f8d6c08c6a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-56","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sojourner Truth was born into slavery in New York around 1797.</li><li>She escaped to freedom in 1826.</li><li>In 1828, she sued to recover her young son, who had been illegally sold into slavery in Alabama.</li><li>She won the case.</li><li>She was one of the first Black women to win such a case against a white man in a U.S. court.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize the historical significance of Truth's lawsuit.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In 1828, two years after escaping to freedom, Sojourner Truth sued to recover her young son, who had been illegally sold into slavery in Alabama.","Sojourner Truth, who was born into slavery in New York around 1797 and escaped in 1826, won a lawsuit in 1828.","When Sojourner Truth won her 1828 lawsuit to recover her son, she became one of the first Black women to win such a case against a white man in a U.S. court.","Sojourner Truth sued to recover her son, who had been illegally sold, and won the case."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Historical significance comes from Truth being one of the first Black women to win such a case against a white man. The other choices describe the lawsuit or her life without stating why the lawsuit was historically significant."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.8383258c68","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-57","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Researchers compared two methods of teaching fractions to fourth graders.</li><li>Method A used number lines.</li><li>Method B used pie charts.</li><li>Students taught with Method A scored 15 percent higher on a test of comparing fractions.</li><li>The two groups scored about the same on a test of adding fractions.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present a limitation of Method A's advantage.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Students taught with number lines, a method known as Method A, scored 15 percent higher than students taught with pie charts on a test of comparing fractions.","Researchers compared two methods of teaching fractions to fourth graders: one using number lines and one using pie charts.","Although students taught with number lines scored higher on comparing fractions, they did no better than students taught with pie charts at adding fractions.","On a test of adding fractions, students taught with pie charts scored about the same as students taught with number lines."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A limitation of Method A's advantage requires both the advantage and where it stops. The correct choice states that number lines helped with comparing but not with adding. The choice about pie charts on the adding test mentions the equal scores but not the advantage they limit. The others describe the study or the advantage alone."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.f4d0adeefd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-58","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Voyager Golden Records were launched on two spacecraft in 1977.</li><li>They contain sounds and images meant to represent life on Earth.</li><li>They include greetings in 55 languages.</li><li>They include music ranging from Bach to Chuck Berry.</li><li>The records were designed to last a billion years.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize the diversity of the records' contents.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Voyager Golden Records include greetings in 55 languages and music ranging from Bach to Chuck Berry.","Launched in 1977, the Voyager Golden Records contain sounds and images meant to represent life on Earth.","Designed to last a billion years, the Voyager Golden Records were launched aboard two spacecraft in 1977 to represent life on Earth.","The Voyager Golden Records, launched on two spacecraft in 1977, were designed to last for a billion years."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Diversity of contents is shown by the range of languages and musical styles. The other choices describe the launch, purpose, or durability, not the variety of the contents."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.5b1e560775","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-59","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tardigrades are microscopic animals.</li><li>They can survive being frozen, dried out, and exposed to high radiation.</li><li>When dried out, they enter a state called cryptobiosis.</li><li>In this state, their metabolism nearly stops.</li><li>Some tardigrades have been revived after decades in cryptobiosis.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain how tardigrades survive drying out.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Tardigrades, microscopic animals that can survive being frozen, dried out, and exposed to high radiation, are among Earth's toughest creatures.","When dried out, tardigrades enter cryptobiosis, a state in which their metabolism nearly stops.","Some tardigrades have been revived after spending decades in a state called cryptobiosis.","Microscopic animals called tardigrades can survive being frozen, dried out, and exposed to high radiation."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Explaining how they survive drying requires the mechanism: entering cryptobiosis, where metabolism nearly stops. The revival choice shows how long they can survive but not how. The other choices say that they survive without explaining how."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.e8bbe1817f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-60","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Taj Mahal is a mausoleum in Agra, India.</li><li>Emperor Shah Jahan commissioned it in 1631.</li><li>It honors his wife Mumtaz Mahal.</li><li>Its white marble changes color depending on the light.</li><li>It appears pinkish in the morning and golden at sunset.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe a visual quality of the Taj Mahal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Emperor Shah Jahan commissioned the Taj Mahal in 1631 to honor his wife.","Commissioned in 1631 by Emperor Shah Jahan, the Taj Mahal is a white marble mausoleum in Agra, India, honoring his wife Mumtaz Mahal.","The Taj Mahal's white marble changes color with the light, appearing pinkish in the morning and golden at sunset.","The Taj Mahal, a mausoleum in Agra, India, honors Mumtaz Mahal, the wife of Emperor Shah Jahan."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A visual quality is shown by how the marble's color shifts with the light. The other choices give history and purpose; mentioning \"white marble\" alone doesn't describe the changing appearance."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.c75ceadbb2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-61","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>In 1954, Roger Bannister ran a mile in 3 minutes 59.4 seconds.</li><li>He was the first person recorded to run a mile in under four minutes.</li><li>Many experts had believed a sub-four-minute mile was physically impossible.</li><li>John Landy broke Bannister's record just 46 days later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to suggest that Bannister's achievement changed beliefs about what was possible.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Although many experts had believed a sub-four-minute mile was impossible, Bannister achieved one in 1954, and John Landy broke his record just 46 days later.","In 1954, Roger Bannister became the first person recorded to run a mile in under four minutes, finishing in 3 minutes 59.4 seconds.","John Landy broke Roger Bannister's record for the mile just 46 days after Bannister set it in 1954.","Roger Bannister ran a mile in 3 minutes 59.4 seconds in 1954."],"answer":"A","explanation":"To suggest a change in beliefs, the choice needs the earlier belief (impossible) and evidence that it changed (Bannister's run, quickly followed by another). The other choices report the times and records without the belief that was overturned."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.5d33398a87","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-62","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kudzu is a vine native to Japan and China.</li><li>It was introduced to the United States in 1876.</li><li>In the 1930s, the U.S. government encouraged farmers to plant it to control soil erosion.</li><li>Kudzu can grow up to 30 centimeters per day.</li><li>It now covers millions of acres in the southeastern United States, smothering native plants.</li></ul><p>The student wants to show how a well-intended action had unintended consequences.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In the 1930s, the U.S. government encouraged farmers to plant kudzu, a vine native to Japan and China, to control soil erosion.","Kudzu now covers millions of acres in the southeastern United States, where it smothers native plants.","Farmers were encouraged to plant kudzu in the 1930s to control soil erosion, but the fast-growing vine now covers millions of acres in the Southeast, smothering native plants.","Kudzu, a vine native to Japan and China that was introduced to the United States in 1876, can grow up to 30 centimeters per day."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal requires both the well-intended action (planting kudzu to control erosion) and its unintended result (smothering native plants). Only the correct choice includes both. The others give only the action, only the result, or background facts."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.0a8f1ad988","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-63","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Great Molasses Flood occurred in Boston in 1919.</li><li>A storage tank holding 8.7 million liters of molasses burst.</li><li>A wave of molasses up to 7.5 meters high swept through the streets.</li><li>The disaster killed 21 people.</li><li>Investigators found that the tank had been poorly constructed and rarely inspected.</li></ul><p>The student wants to explain the cause of the disaster.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Great Molasses Flood occurred when a poorly constructed, rarely inspected storage tank burst.","The Great Molasses Flood, which occurred in Boston in 1919, killed 21 people.","In 1919, a wave of molasses up to 7.5 meters high swept through the streets of Boston, killing 21 people.","A storage tank in Boston holding 8.7 million liters of molasses burst in 1919, and the resulting flood killed 21 people."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The cause includes both the tank bursting and why it failed: poor construction and rare inspection. The storage-tank choice mentions the burst but not why it happened. The other choices describe the effects."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.3.95a2f05e22","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-SY","group":"hard-hard_data6-64","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Bioluminescence is the production of light by living things.</li><li>Fireflies use bioluminescence to attract mates.</li><li>Anglerfish use a glowing lure to attract prey.</li><li>Some squid use bioluminescence to match light from above and hide their silhouettes from predators below.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present examples showing that bioluminescence serves different purposes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Bioluminescence, the production of light by living things, is used by fireflies, anglerfish, and some squid.","Fireflies use bioluminescence to attract mates, anglerfish use it to attract prey, and some squid use it to hide from predators.","Some squid use bioluminescence to match light from above, hiding their silhouettes from predators below.","Anglerfish use a glowing lure to attract prey, and fireflies also glow."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal needs examples with different purposes. Only the correct choice pairs each organism with its distinct use. The definition choice lists organisms without purposes. The anglerfish/firefly choice gives only one purpose, and the squid choice gives only one example."}
]);
